{
  "algorithm": "phash-dct-64-v2",
  "description": "Golden 32x32 luma matrix -> phash-dct-64-v2 digest vectors for the encoding frozen in contracts/schemas/common.schema.json (issue #14, finding 2). The input is the luma matrix and not an image file, because the resample-and-grey step is NOT reproducible across imaging libraries -- the image crate greys with Rec. 709 weights and integer division, Pillow with Rec. 601 -- so a file-level vector would freeze a number no second implementation could reproduce. Everything after the matrix is exactly determined, and that is what these vectors pin. `legacy_phash_dct_64_hex` records what the superseded algorithm produced on the same input, so the difference between the two is committed rather than described.",
  "side": 32,
  "luma_rows_hex": "32 rows of 64 lowercase hex characters, one byte per column, row 0 at the top; luma[y][x] with x the column index.",
  "coefficient_order": "(u, v) row-major over 0 <= u < 8, 0 <= v < 8 with (0, 0) omitted, then (0, 8) appended. Position 0 in this list is bit 63, the most significant bit of the digest.",
  "threshold": "the element at index 32 of the 64 coefficient values sorted ascending; a bit is 1 if and only if its coefficient is STRICTLY greater. At most 31 bits can therefore be set.",
  "why_no_flat_or_separable_inputs": "A blank field, a linear ramp and a checkerboard are all separable in x and y, which drives 48 to 64 of the 64 coefficients to within 1e-10 of zero and puts the threshold inside that cloud -- so most of the digest would be decided by floating-point summation order rather than by the picture. Committing one would pin rounding error. Every vector here clears 0.001 on its smallest coefficient and 1e-06 on the threshold gap, both recorded per vector so the margin is checkable rather than claimed. The degenerate inputs are covered by property assertions in contracts/tests and by issue-tracked behaviour, not by golden digests.",
